     No, it's just a side-effect of attempting to fix a very long-
standing bug where getting interrupted while eating any food which
takes more than one bite sometimes left an untouched piece of food
(instead of a partially eaten one) and at other times left partly
eaten food which became massively heavy and produced an outrageously
high degree of satiation if eaten.  Any zero nutrition food (wraith
corpse might be the only actual instance of that) will now exhibit
this new (as of 3.4.0, I think, so not so new any more) behavior if
you're interrupted while eating--none of the food will be left for
you to try to resume eating it.  Effectively, there's no stage
between "zero" and "all gone" for such a corpse to end up in.

     It has nothing whatever to do with the wraith corpses rotting
quickly or with wraith monsters being undead.  A wraith corpse lasts
just as long as the corpse of any ordinary monster provided you're
not attempting to eat it.  All food (except the couple of types of
corpses which never rot away) has a random chance of being "rotten"
when you try to eat it.  That yields a result which is similar to
being interrupted, more or less, so every wraith corpse ends up
having a 1/7 chance of being inedible.


